# T0 — the commit gate. Target: under 10 s. Design:
# docs/strategy/2026-09-13-gate-tiering-design.md §1.
#
# verify:lint stays WHOLE and unscoped. It is the only layer that reads
# CHANGELOG.md, CLAUDE.md, the version faces, the protocol whitelist and the
# sibling-repo mirrors, and scoping 40 checks would mean each of them declaring
# which roots it reads — which most of them do not, because they walk the whole
# tree (verify/lint/file-size.mjs, verify/lint/no-cloud-keys.mjs).
#
# verify:scripts is NOT here — it is ~11 s, which does not fit a commit hook —
# but note that it is unconditional one layer up, in `pnpm verify:lane` (ruling
# MAIN 2026-09-13). So a change to apps/server-core/src/compose/output-guard.ts
# still meets its resident gate (scripts/w2-eval-corpus.test.mjs:147) at T1,
# just not at T0.
#
# Types: incremental, and only the packages this commit touches. Replaces
# `pnpm verify:types` (all four packages from cold, measured 23.4 s in
# docs/archive/strategy/2026-09-12-verify-delivery-speedup-plan.md §1.2).
#
# 🔴 PATH A, KEPT. Measured, then decided, then decided again — and the second
# decision is the one that stands (owner's priority this round is quality
# first).
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-13 on dev-pc-a, worst case (a one-line comment staged in
# apps/server-core/src/, the slowest of the four projects), warm buildinfo,
# `git commit` end to end, sampled only in windows where neither the flutter
# nor the golden lane held the machine lock (a run where a lock appeared
# mid-flight was discarded and re-taken):
#
#     run   total     types   lint
#     m1    11086ms   3.4s    3292ms
#     m2    11360ms   4.3s    4314ms
#     m3     7130ms   1.9s    3170ms
#     median 11086ms  3.4s
#
# THE TYPE CHECK MET ITS OWN BUDGET: median 3.4 s against 4 s. The 10 s total
# was a target, and it is over by 1.1 s.
#
# ⚠️ AND ONE MORE READING, taken after the decision, on a QUIET box (both lane
# locks free, no flutter_tester, no cargo): the same worst-case commit went
# through in 7835 ms end to end — lint 3282 ms, types 2.0 s (server-core only,
# incremental). So the 11.1 s median above was measured while two other lanes
# were working the machine, and the hook is inside the 10 s target when it is
# not competing. Both numbers are kept: the median is the honest pessimistic
# one, and this is the honest typical one. Neither is a promise.
#
# 🔴 WHY THAT OVERSHOOT DOES NOT BUY A CHECK'S REMOVAL. Of the 11.1 s median,
# lint reports 3.3 s of its own and types 3.4 s. The remaining ~4.4 s is not
# verification at all: two `pnpm` launches, husky, and the commit-msg hook's
# own node start, on Windows. Deleting the type check would pay for process
# startup with coverage — the wrong side of the trade, and the one that is hard
# to notice later, because the hook would still look fast and still say "ok".
#
# 🔴 SC-10 (2026-09-17/18), BUILT: the honest lever on that 4.4 s WAS running
# the whole hook as ONE node process instead of two pnpm launches plus a node —
# the paragraph above used to end "OPEN ITEM, NOT BUILT HERE" and now does not,
# because verify/precommit-single.mjs is that: it imports verify/lint/run-all.mjs's
# main() and verify/precommit-types.mjs's main() and awaits them in this one
# process, fail-fast (lint first; a nonzero result returns immediately without
# running the type check, matching `sh -e`'s old two-line semantics exactly —
# see verify/precommit-single.mjs's own header). SAME STAGE SET, SAME
# skip/pass OUTPUT LINES — only the process count changes: 2 -> 1. It removes
# no coverage.
#
# MEASURED (dev-pc-a, 2026-09-17/18), three real `git commit`s each (a
# throwaway scratch file, reset away afterwards — not this diff), timed with
# the shell's own `time` around `git commit`, end to end. This session's box
# was NOT quiet — other verify runs were in flight on the same machine, so
# read these against EACH OTHER, not against the 2026-09-13 quiet-box 7835 ms
# reading above:
#
#   two-process (`pnpm verify:lint` then `node verify/precommit-types.mjs`), BEFORE:
#     docs-only touch    6760ms / 7540ms / 6867ms   → median 6867ms
#     server-core touch 18077ms / 17278ms / 23911ms → median 18077ms
#   one process (verify/precommit-single.mjs), AFTER:
#     docs-only touch    5133ms / 7931ms / 7288ms   → median 7288ms
#     server-core touch  8930ms / 8996ms / 8488ms   → median 8930ms
#
# THE TS-TOUCH CASE IMPROVED CLEARLY: median 18077ms -> 8930ms, roughly halved
# — this is where both stages' own work (lint AND tsc) previously paid for a
# SECOND `pnpm`+`node` startup on top of the first, and now do not.
#
# 🔴 THE DOCS-ONLY CASE DID NOT MOVE (6867ms -> 7288ms is noise, not a
# regression) — say so rather than round it into the win. Removing one process
# launch should save something on this path too, and it may be masked by this
# box being busier during the AFTER samples than the BEFORE ones (both sets
# were taken back to back, not interleaved — a confound this reading does not
# rule out). Neither AFTER median meets the design's ≤7s target for a
# ts-touch, and this box was under load throughout — a quiet-box re-read,
# INTERLEAVED before/after rather than blocked, is the next honest step
# before trusting an absolute number over this session's directional one.
#
# desktop vue-tsc stays OUT of this gate: it is the slow one (vue-tsc over the
# whole SFC graph). It runs in `pnpm verify:lane` (verify:types:desktop) and in
# both full gates — stated here so the gap is visible rather than assumed
# covered.
node verify/precommit-single.mjs
